From Nette Coding Standard to DressCode
***************************************

.[perex]
Nette Coding Standard, the `nette/coding-standard` package with its `ecs` command, is no longer developed, and version 3 is the last one. Its standard lives on in DressCode, built in under the name `nette`. Moving a project, `ncs.php` and `ncs.xml` included, takes a few lines of configuration, and the only surprise is the first fix, which reformats more files than you expect.


What Has Changed
================

Nette Coding Standard was a thin wrapper around PHP CS Fixer and PHP_CodeSniffer with a bundle of rules from Slevomat: two tools, two configurations (`ncs.php` for the fixer, `ncs.xml` for the sniffer) and the `ecs` command that ran it all.

In DressCode, the standard is a **preset**. It is called `nette` and is part of DressCode, so every project has it available, whether it uses Nette or not. It is based on PER Coding Style, which it adjusts to the layout of the Nette libraries (tabs, two blank lines between methods and more), and it also uses the [sets |configuration#Standards and Sets] `cleanup`, `types` and `correctness`, turning off a few of their decisions; exactly what it contains is shown in the [overview of standards and sets |php-coding-standards]. Nothing else is needed:

- no package besides DressCode itself; `nette/coding-standard` stays at version 3 and you remove it from your project;
- no command besides `dresscode`; `ecs` is not used anymore.


Installation and Configuration
==============================

```shell
composer require --dev dresscode/dresscode
```

Keep in mind that DressCode requires PHP 8.4 to 8.6, and because it is a development dependency, your project must run on one of these versions too. The PHP version the tool runs on and the version your code is written for are two different numbers, though: DressCode reads the target version from the `composer.json` of the project, see [Installation |getting-started#Installation]. If your project runs on an older PHP, install DressCode globally instead.

Put a `dresscode.neon` into the root of the project, with the standard and the extra sets you want:

```neon
use:
	- nette
	- compilerOptimizations   # optional

nameResolution: certain

# optional: native types wherever PHP reads them
types:
	declaration:
		parameter: required
		property: required
		return: required

paths:
	- src
	- tests
```

Version 3 skipped the directories `expected`, `tmp` and `fixtures*` automatically. DressCode skips `tmp` too; add the others to `excludePaths` if your project has them, for example `excludePaths: [tests/**/expected, tests/**/fixtures*]`.

Some fixes rely on a function being global, including what the [`compilerOptimizations` |php-functions-in-namespaces#Faster Calls When the Name Is Certain] set does. DressCode makes them without your permission only when it knows what is in your namespaces. That is why the configuration says `nameResolution: certain`, and if you really have some functions or constants in namespaces, you also list them in the `namespaces` key; without that, such fixes are only reported. The page [Functions and Constants in Namespaces |php-functions-in-namespaces] explains why.

Then you check and fix with DressCode itself:

```shell
dresscode check
dresscode fix
```

A setting that differs from version 3 only in its value is written as a decision. A project that wants one blank line between methods instead of two, and does not want `===` everywhere there is `==`, adds:

```neon
blankLines:
	aroundMethods: 1

expressions:
	comparison:
		loose: keep
```


Converting the Configuration
============================

There is no command that converts `ncs.php` and `ncs.xml`, but they are usually a few lines long, so you carry them over by hand. To find the decision that replaces a fixer or a sniff of version 3, search for its name in [`dresscode catalogue` |configuration#All Decisions the Installation Knows: dresscode catalogue]:

```shell
dresscode catalogue | grep strict_comparison
```

It prints the decision, what it is about, and the names of the rules of other tools it covers: `expressions.comparison.loose  A loose comparison, ...  (covers strict_comparison, SlevomatCodingStandard.Operators.DisallowEqualOperators)`.

| version 3 | DressCode |
|---|---|
| `ncs.php` with `'fixer' => false` | the decision the fixer covers, with the value `keep` |
| `ncs.xml` with `<rule ref="$presets/optimize-fn.xml"/>` | the `compilerOptimizations` set in the `use` key |
| `ncs.xml` with `<exclude-pattern>` | the `excludePaths` key |
| `ncs.xml` with `<exclude-pattern>` inside `<rule>` | an override in the `overrides` key that gives the decision `keep` for those paths |
| `--config-file overrides.php` | `dresscode.neon` in the root of the project |
| `--preset php81`, `--preset php` | nothing; DressCode takes the target PHP version from your `composer.json` |

Then replace `ecs check` and `ecs fix` with `dresscode check` and `dresscode fix` in your CI, Git hooks and editor, uninstall `nette/coding-standard`, and delete `ncs.php` and `ncs.xml`.


The First Fix Reformats More Than You Expect
============================================

DressCode is not the same as version 3 line by line, and that is on purpose. Some differences fix what version 3 left alone: a capital letter at the start of a comment `// Comment`, a `/* enum */` comment before `case`, `declare(strict_types=1)` on the line of the opening tag, a non-breaking space written literally in a string. Across the Nette packages, this came to more than a hundred files out of almost three thousand.

Make it a separate commit: `dresscode fix`, a commit with no other changes, done. Going through it line by line is not worth it; what is worth it is knowing that the commit contains nothing else.


What Has Stayed
===============

- The `phpcs:ignore` comments with the names of PHP_CodeSniffer and Slevomat sniffs keep working; [dresscode migrate suppressions |migration#3. Rewrite the Comments] rewrites them to `dresscode:ignore`.
- Custom sniffs and fixers, if there were any, are rewritten following the [guide |porting-rules]; a rewritten sniff is usually several times shorter.

